12:15:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That reputation is real, but it's worth unpacking why it exists and how outdated it actually is as a picture of the country. The famine imagery you're thinking of is mostly from 1983-85 — that's 40 years ago now. It became iconic partly because of Live Aid and the sheer scale of Western media coverage, and that imagery got recycled in charity advertising for decades afterward because it was effective at generating donations, not because it reflected ongoing conditions. That's a real problem with a lot of humanitarian marketing: it freezes a country in a moment of crisis and keeps using that image long after the situation has changed, because it works better for fundraising than "actually things have improved a lot, but there are still specific problems in specific areas." Since the 80s, Ethiopia has had one of the fastest-growing economies in Africa for stretches of the 2000s-2010s, massive infrastructure investment, urbanization, and Addis Ababa becoming a genuine African diplomatic and business hub (it's headquarters for the African Union). That doesn't mean poverty and food insecurity don't exist — they do, especially in rural areas and in regions hit by drought or conflict — but "the whole country is starving" was never accurate even in the 80s (famine was concentrated in specific regions, notably Tigray and Wollo, driven by a combination of drought and the Derg government's policies) and is much less accurate now. The "what a country is known for worldwide" thing is also just a function of what gets covered. Ethiopia rarely makes Western news except during crises — Tigray war coverage, drought coverage — so people's mental image gets built entirely from bad news, while the ordinary functioning of the country (which is most of it, most of the time) never appears at all. That's a real bias in how international news works, not a reflection of proportions on the ground. So I'd say: your instinct that this is "what the country is known for" is accurate as a statement about Western perception. It's just not a reliable guide to what you'll actually encounter.
