12:17:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's an intuitive assumption, but it doesn't actually hold up — poverty and hunger are related but distinct, and conflating them will give you a distorted picture. Being "dirt poor" typically means lacking cash income, adequate housing, shoes, healthcare, education — material deprivation broadly. It does not automatically mean insufficient caloric intake. A huge share of Ethiopia's poor are subsistence farmers who grow their own food (teff, maize, sorghum) — they may have almost no cash income and go without shoes or modern goods, while still eating enough calories most of the year. Their poverty shows up as lack of money, not necessarily lack of food. The actual data backs this up: Ethiopia's undernourishment rate has dropped substantially since the 1980s — organizations like the Global Hunger Index now categorize Ethiopia's hunger level as "serious," not "alarming" or "extremely alarming" (which is where actual famine-level countries like Yemen or South Sudan in crisis years land). Chronic child stunting is a real problem — meaning inadequate nutrition over time — but that's different again from acute starvation, and it's not visually obvious to a tourist walking around. On begging specifically: someone begging near a tourist site in Ethiopia is often doing so as one income stream among several, and targeting foreigners because it's a rational economic strategy (tourists are perceived as wealthy and generous), not because it's their only source of food. It tells you they're poor and see an opportunity, not that they're starving. So: no shoes and begging = real, significant poverty, absolutely. It's not a reliable signal of famine-level hunger, and treating it as equivalent will lead you to overestimate the crisis-level severity of what you're seeing, even though the underlying poverty is genuinely serious and worth taking seriously on its own terms.
