ecause it's falsifiable and updated, rather than transmitted socially and repeated. To be clear, I'm not saying "ignore what you were taught, trust me instead." I'm saying: the fact that a claim has survived 40 years of retelling is weak evidence on its own, and you have better evidence available — direct data, recent traveler accounts (including the poverty your friends described, which was real), and the specific breakdown of what's chronic poverty vs. acute famine. Those should carry more weight than "this is the story I've heard since childhood," even though I get why that story feels authoritative from sheer repetition.
